# The owner's canonical map is deliberately a plain blob, not an LFS pointer (run 361, ADR-0308).
# Every geography gate reads its actual pixels -- checkRegionGeographyFidelity, checkMapFeatureFidelity,
# checkValyriaDoom, checkOwnerMapAndRoadRoutes and others -- and an LFS-tracked file is only a 130-byte
# pointer until someone hydrates it. Tracking this one in LFS would mean those gates silently measure a
# pointer stub in any environment that has not run `git lfs pull`, which is exactly the failure mode
# RCA_RUN344 recorded for the model catalogue. Committed raw at 667 KB, it is always readable.
#
# Without this exemption the file contradicts the `*.png` rule above and `git lfs fsck` rejects the tree
# ("resimler/map.png should have been a pointer but was not"), which is what broke the final-head-gate.
# `map.png/map.png` (the owner's own upload, PR #792) keeps the LFS rule and stays a pointer; nothing
# loads it, and scripts/checkOwnerMapAndRoadRoutes.js compares it against this one when it is hydrated.
resimler/map.png -filter -diff -merge -text

# Run 416 — the ground's micro-surface tiles, cropped out of the owner's own `dirt_road_test.glb`
# (its baked normal and albedo atlas) and committed as plain blobs for exactly the reason
# `resimler/map.png` above is: the renderer has to read their real pixels, and an LFS-tracked file is a
# 130-byte pointer in any environment that has not run `git lfs pull` — including this one, which has no
# git-lfs binary at all. Tracked in LFS they would render as broken textures rather than as ground.
# All three are 128x128 and together under 100 KB; they are the only image assets this rule exempts
# besides the canonical map.
assets/textures/ground/ground_grain_normal.png -filter -diff -merge -text
assets/textures/ground/ground_grain_albedo.png -filter -diff -merge -text
assets/textures/roads/road_verge_grass.png -filter -diff -merge -text
